  {
    "episode_id": 70869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Today's guest is Seth Moulton, a congressman from Massachusetts, and the leader of an effort to get other veterans like him, who was a captain in the Marines, enlisted after graduating Harvard, who served four tours of duty in Iraq, earning the Bronze Star long away. He's someone who's gotten Democratic insiders all over the country, talking about him in a way that doesn't quite make sense for a guy who's only been in office since 2014, when he beat a long time incumbent in a Democratic primary, and then held the seat despite an intense race against a Republican. You may know Molten from leading a charge against Nancy Pelosi, trying to topple her as leader. Where you may know him from some of the media speculation about his future that he stoked. From that and other moves, like taking a trip to Iowa last year. I figured, let's hear it directly from the man himself. Since I asked him, as you'll hear, about all the different things his future could hold, and the only thing he ruled out was running for Pelosi's job himself. Molten this week is announcing a major expansion of the candidates he's endorsing through his group, Serve America. He'll be backing not just military veterans, but veterans of other service as well, like AmeriCorps, and backing candidates for races beyond Congress. That's all in the story that's up on the website over at politico.com, which includes me talking with some of the candidates he's backed and why they think it matters to have his endorsement and why they're so excited that he's come to campaign for many of them in a spike of stops that are also starting this week. He's going to New York, Michigan, and Ohio, and there's going to be much more. Another thing you'll read in there is getting at how Molten manages to be a hero to some, but drives other people, particularly people in power in Washington, absolutely nuts. So please get on the site and check that out. But I want to talk about something else that happened in this interview. Some of you, hopefully a lot of you, may have read an article I wrote for Politico two weeks ago about Barack Obama meaning with perspective 2020 candidates, among others, and giving his advice about how they should approach the years ahead and think about their own plans. Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth Warren, Deval Patrick, Joe Biden, even Eric Garcetti and Pete Buttigieg have been in. I wrote then that Obama had met with at least nine people who might run because I thought there was a chance that I might have missed some of my reporting then. But, Mulden was not on my list. I thought from what I had been told he hadn't been in. And that's the understanding I came into this conversation with. Well guys, one of the things that I like about these podcast conversations, and I hope you agree, is that you can hear the reporting going on as I'm doing it. And toward the end, you'll hear me say to Molten that my reporting was that he hadn't been in. And Molten say back to me that I should check my reporting. So I did. And he was right. Molten met with Obama in June 2017, which actually makes him one of the first politicians who was in to see Obama after he left the White House. before Bernie Sanders was in or Mitch Landrieu or almost any of the others. My guess is that I missed this either because it happened so far back or because people hadn't been thinking of him as a 2020 candidate. But the point is that my reporting in that article was incomplete and the way I talked about it with him in here was not correct. But that's what we're prodding is, and what it should be. We go out to get the most accurate information we can. We ask as many people as we can, and hopefully by the time we get to the end of the process, we're as right as we can be. Along the way, there are often moments like these, which hopefully get cleared up like this one did by the time it's public. And that's why I decided to leave that part in, rather than chop it out, when no one but Molten would be the wiser. Curious what you think about all this? Email me your thoughts about this or anything else with the interview at Isaac at Politico.com or guest suggestions. I try to write back to everyone. Remember also to subscribe and tell everyone you know to subscribe on Apple Podcasts, Stitcher, or however you're listening. All those subscriptions help get off message out to more and more people. As to all the reviews and ratings you leave, so please do that, too. Upcoming episodes are going to include presidential historian Kate Anderson-Brower, Boston Mayor Marty Walsh, Miami Mayor Francis Suarez, and many more. You're not going to want to miss any of them. And follow me on Twitter at Isaac Dauver for more from this interview and all sorts of other things I'm working on beyond the podcast. And now, my conversation with Seth Moulton. Can we start by talking about your coffee thing? I was going to ask you about milk, you drink milk.",
