    "turn_text": "Well, I think that fundamentally they have some issues with the military that just came from the 1960s. They don't anymore. They've seen how good it's been for me and how proud I am to have served. But at the time, when I was looking to sign up, like most parents, they were just concerned. They didn't want to see me get hurt. And they didn't think that joining the military was something that would be helpful to me. And it could be very harmful. How old are you when you start having this discussion with them? In college. I wasn't interested in the military before. I wasn't particularly interested in growing up or anything like that. I spent a lot of time in my college church. The memorial church, as it's called, was built to honor the veterans who died in World War I. Where are we talking? at Harvard. And it's right in the center of the yard. And the church was designed so that when students were coming to service, the students would all enter through a separate entrance right through the World War I memorial to constantly be reminded of that sacrifice. And then about 10 years after it was built, they had to add a massive marble wall for World War II. And you can't sit in that church every Sunday and not think about the young men on that wall.",
